  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>The photo on your phone is personal data. So is the name on the job sheet. UK GDPR applies from the moment you collect it. Here is what to do.</p><p><strong>This page is general information, not legal advice.</strong> Ask a solicitor, or the Information Commission at <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk\">ico.org.uk</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>Most field reports hold personal data. Names, addresses, photos.</li><li>Most organisations that process personal data must pay the data protection fee to the Information Commission. Tier 1 is £52 a year (£47 by direct debit).</li><li>Pick a lawful basis. Contract or legitimate interests. Rarely consent.</li><li>Keep data no longer than you need. Write a retention policy, even one page.</li><li>Where data is hosted matters. UK rules treat the EEA as adequate for transfers.</li><li>Tell people what you collect. A short notice is enough.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "What counts",
      "heading": "Your reports hold personal data",
      "html": "<p>Personal data is anything about a person who can be identified. A name. An address linked to a person. A photo where someone is recognisable.</p><p>The format does not matter. Paper, PDF, cloud photo or voice note.</p><p>Common examples: a householder's address, an occupier's signature, names of staff, photos with people in them. Data about a company is usually not personal data. A sole trader under their own name is the usual exception.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step one",
      "heading": "Register with the Information Commission. It takes ten minutes",
      "html": "<p>Most organisations that handle personal data must pay the Information Commission's yearly data protection fee. Tier 1 (up to 10 staff and turnover of £632,000 or less) is £52 a year, or £47 by direct debit, set by the Data Protection (Charges and Information) Regulations 2018 as amended from 17 February 2025.</p><p>The Regulations exempt some organisations, where all their processing is exempt processing. Check the Commission's self-assessment before you assume you are in or out. <strong>Not paying the fee can lead to a penalty notice from the Commission.</strong></p><p>The Information Commission has a self-check at <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/data-protection-fee/\">ico.org.uk</a>. Registering is only the start. The other rules apply either way.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step two",
      "heading": "Pick a lawful basis. Skip consent",
      "html": "<p>You need a lawful basis for everything you do with personal data.</p><p><strong>Contract.</strong> You need the data to do the job for your client. That covers a client's name and address.</p><p><strong>Legitimate interests.</strong> You need it for your own proper aims, and it does not harm the person. Safety, compliance and admin records usually fit.</p><p>Consent is rarely right for field data. It is hard to rely on, and people can withdraw it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Step three",
      "heading": "Keep it no longer than you need",
      "html": "<p>There is no one number for field reports. It depends on why you made the report.</p><ul><li><strong>Legal records</strong> such as RIDDOR, gas safety and EICR have their own minimum times. Keep them at least that long.</li><li><strong>Contract records</strong>: the Limitation Act 1980 gives six years for simple contract claims (section 5) and twelve for deeds (section 8) in England and Wales. Data protection law sets no number, so be ready to justify the period you pick.</li><li><strong>Safety records</strong>: the legal minimum if there is one, or six years.</li></ul><p>A one-page retention policy shows you thought about it.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Where it lives",
      "heading": "Hosting matters",
      "html": "<p>UK GDPR limits sending personal data outside the UK without safeguards. UK adequacy regulations cover the European Economic Area, so a transfer from the UK to an EU recipient needs no extra transfer safeguards, though the Commission advises reasonable checks on the recipient.</p><p>So ask any tool: where does the data sit?</p><p>Quickler keeps all data on Hetzner servers in Germany (EU). It is registered with the Information Commission, ZC124993. A Data Processing Agreement is available on request.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Transparency",
      "heading": "Tell people what you collect",
      "html": "<p>Tell people how you use their data, at or before you collect it. Say who you are, why, what for, how long you keep it, and how they can use their rights.</p><p>A short paragraph on the job sheet, or a link to your privacy policy, is enough. The Information Commission has templates. Want to see it in action? <a href=\"/pages/demo.html\">See how it works.</a></p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Sources",
      "heading": "Official sources",
      "html": "<p>The data protection fee is set by the Data Protection (Charges and Information) Regulations 2018. After the amendment in force from 17 February 2025, tier 1 is £52, tier 2 is £78 and tier 3 is £3,763, each £5 less by direct debit. Tier 1 covers organisations with up to 10 staff and turnover of £632,000 or less. Since 30 September 2026 the regulator is the Information Commission, which replaced the Information Commissioner's Office as the legal body (it continues to use the ICO name). Contract claims in England and Wales have a six year limit and deed claims twelve years. UK transfers to the EEA are covered by UK adequacy regulations, as set out in the Commission's international transfers guidance. We could not read the Commission's own fee page (it blocks automated reading), so the amounts above come from legislation.gov.uk. Official sources checked on 18 November 2026.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2018/480/regulation/3\" rel=\"noopener\">Data Protection (Charges and Information) Regulations 2018, regulation 3: the charge (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2018/12/section/137\" rel=\"noopener\">Data Protection Act 2018, section 137: charges payable to the Commission (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1980/58/section/5\" rel=\"noopener\">Limitation Act 1980, section 5: simple contract, six years (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1980/58/section/8\" rel=\"noopener\">Limitation Act 1980, section 8: specialty, twelve years (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/\" rel=\"noopener\">International transfers (Information Commission)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/data-protection-fee/\" rel=\"noopener\">Data protection fee (Information Commission)</a></li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Do field reports hold personal data?</h3><p>Usually yes. A name, an address linked to a person, or a photo of a person is personal data. It does not matter if it is on paper or digital.</p><h3>Must a small firm register with the Information Commission?</h3><p>Almost all commercial firms must. The fee is £40 a year for a small organisation. Not paying the fee can lead to a penalty notice from the Commission. Check at <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk\">ico.org.uk</a>.</p><h3>How long should I keep field reports?</h3><p>No longer than you need. Legal records have a minimum. Contract claims in England and Wales can be brought for six years (twelve for a deed), so many firms keep contract records that long. The law sets no single number. Write a retention policy.</p><h3>Does EU or UK hosting matter?</h3><p>UK GDPR limits sending data outside the UK without safeguards. UK adequacy regulations cover transfers to the EEA, and the EU adopted renewed adequacy decisions for the UK on 19 December 2025 (valid to 27 December 2031). Quickler uses Hetzner servers in Germany. Quickler's registration number with the Information Commission is ZC124993. DPA on request.</p>"
    }
